1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,235
A ceiling fan spins so fast
that its blades become a blur.

2
00:00:04,235 --> 00:00:06,298
You cannot point to one blade.

3
00:00:06,298 --> 00:00:09,173
You only see where
blades are likely to be.

4
00:00:09,573 --> 00:00:12,107
A weather report never says it will rain.

5
00:00:12,107 --> 00:00:14,686
It says there is a seventy percent chance.

6
00:00:15,086 --> 00:00:18,050
Aarav stared at the fan
above his study table.

7
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:20,468
Where exactly is one blade right now?

8
00:00:20,468 --> 00:00:21,753
He could not say.

9
00:00:22,103 --> 00:00:26,162
An electron in an atom is like
that blur, only far stranger.

10
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:27,958
We can only give chances.

11
00:00:28,458 --> 00:00:32,704
By the end of this video, you will
see how modern chemistry pictures an

12
00:00:32,704 --> 00:00:36,183
electron, not as a dot on
a circle, but as a cloud.

13
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:38,811
So where does this idea come from?

14
00:00:39,161 --> 00:00:42,008
Electrons are waves, as de Broglie showed.

15
00:00:42,008 --> 00:00:46,084
And their exact path can never
be known, as Heisenberg showed.

16
00:00:46,484 --> 00:00:48,858
So Bohr's neat circles had to go.

17
00:00:48,858 --> 00:00:54,194
We needed a new theory for tiny particles
that act as both waves and particles.

18
00:00:54,594 --> 00:00:56,812
That theory is quantum mechanics.

19
00:00:56,812 --> 00:01:00,502
It is the science of the
motion of such tiny particles.

20
00:01:01,102 --> 00:01:05,752
In nineteen twenty six, Erwin
Schrodinger wrote its central equation.

21
00:01:05,752 --> 00:01:08,426
In short form, H psi equals E psi.

22
00:01:08,426 --> 00:01:11,206
E is the total energy of the electron.

23
00:01:11,856 --> 00:01:15,966
Let's see what the equation
gives us, on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:15,966 --> 00:01:17,782
Here is a hydrogen atom.

25
00:01:17,782 --> 00:01:19,692
One proton, one electron.

26
00:01:20,042 --> 00:01:24,596
Solve the equation for this atom,
and out comes a wave function, psi.

27
00:01:24,596 --> 00:01:26,892
Psi itself has no physical meaning.

28
00:01:27,292 --> 00:01:28,776
But psi squared does.

29
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:33,383
Psi squared tells us the probability
of finding the electron at each point.

30
00:01:33,783 --> 00:01:36,673
Now let's look for the
electron again and again.

31
00:01:36,673 --> 00:01:39,149
Each time we find it, we mark a dot.

32
00:01:39,549 --> 00:01:42,127
Near the nucleus, the dots crowd together.

33
00:01:42,127 --> 00:01:43,968
Farther out, they thin away.

34
00:01:43,968 --> 00:01:46,007
They never stop at a sharp edge.

35
00:01:46,407 --> 00:01:48,431
Thousands of dots make a cloud.

36
00:01:48,431 --> 00:01:52,002
Dense where psi squared is
big, faint where it is small.

37
00:01:52,002 --> 00:01:54,120
This cloud picture is an orbital.

38
00:01:54,770 --> 00:01:58,201
Now compare an orbit and
an orbital, side by side.

39
00:01:58,601 --> 00:02:00,682
Bohr's orbit is a fixed circle.

40
00:02:00,682 --> 00:02:04,183
A flat path, with an exact
position at every moment.

41
00:02:04,583 --> 00:02:09,020
An orbital is a three dimensional
region, around the nucleus, where the

42
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:11,444
electron is most likely to be found.

43
00:02:11,844 --> 00:02:13,052
Pause and think.

44
00:02:13,052 --> 00:02:16,953
Can we draw the exact path of
an electron inside an orbital?

45
00:02:20,153 --> 00:02:21,053
No!

46
00:02:20,651 --> 00:02:23,089
An orbital gives only probability.

47
00:02:23,089 --> 00:02:26,416
The uncertainty principle
forbids an exact path.

48
00:02:27,066 --> 00:02:30,591
Let's list what this model
tells us, one link at a time.

49
00:02:30,941 --> 00:02:31,841
Link one.

50
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:35,201
The energy of an electron
in an atom is quantised.

51
00:02:35,201 --> 00:02:37,397
Only certain values are allowed.

52
00:02:38,147 --> 00:02:39,047
Link two.

53
00:02:38,952 --> 00:02:43,033
These allowed energies come from
the wave nature of the electron.

54
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:46,496
They are the allowed solutions
of Schrodinger's equation.

55
00:02:47,246 --> 00:02:48,201
Link three.

56
00:02:48,201 --> 00:02:51,447
Each allowed solution
is a wave function, psi.

57
00:02:51,447 --> 00:02:56,260
A wave function for an electron in
an atom is called an atomic orbital.

58
00:02:57,010 --> 00:02:57,910
Link four.

59
00:02:57,877 --> 00:03:02,343
The probability of finding the electron
at a point is proportional to psi

60
00:03:02,343 --> 00:03:03,409
squared there.

61
00:03:03,409 --> 00:03:07,048
That is why we call psi squared
the probability density.

62
00:03:07,798 --> 00:03:08,748
Link five.

63
00:03:08,748 --> 00:03:12,110
An orbital cannot hold
more than two electrons.

64
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:14,035
Remember this.

65
00:03:14,035 --> 00:03:15,681
An orbit is a path.

66
00:03:15,681 --> 00:03:18,216
An orbital is a probability cloud.

67
00:03:18,916 --> 00:03:20,652
Now, one worked example.

68
00:03:20,952 --> 00:03:24,443
An electron has these psi
values at three points.

69
00:03:24,443 --> 00:03:26,770
Point A, plus zero point six.

70
00:03:26,770 --> 00:03:29,259
Point B, minus zero point eight.

71
00:03:29,259 --> 00:03:31,587
Point C, plus zero point two.

72
00:03:32,087 --> 00:03:34,996
Where is the electron
most likely to be found?

73
00:03:35,496 --> 00:03:36,689
Square each one.

74
00:03:36,689 --> 00:03:38,688
A gives zero point three six.

75
00:03:39,438 --> 00:03:41,464
B gives zero point six four.

76
00:03:41,464 --> 00:03:44,095
A minus sign, squared, becomes plus.

77
00:03:44,845 --> 00:03:47,422
C gives only zero point zero four.

78
00:03:48,172 --> 00:03:52,312
So point B is the most likely,
even though its psi is negative.

79
00:03:52,312 --> 00:03:54,294
Psi squared is never negative.

80
00:03:54,944 --> 00:03:56,954
So what did the new model fix?

81
00:03:57,354 --> 00:04:00,826
Bohr's model ignored the
wave nature of the electron.

82
00:04:00,826 --> 00:04:05,405
It also gave the electron an exact
path, which the uncertainty principle

83
00:04:05,405 --> 00:04:06,305
rules out.

84
00:04:06,697 --> 00:04:11,165
The quantum mechanical model keeps
the good part, quantised energy, and

85
00:04:11,165 --> 00:04:13,259
replaces paths with probability.

86
00:04:13,259 --> 00:04:15,734
That is the atom chemists use today.

87
00:04:16,384 --> 00:04:17,850
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:22,809
Many students write that psi gives the
probability of finding the electron.

89
00:04:23,209 --> 00:04:24,109
Wrong.

90
00:04:23,818 --> 00:04:27,721
Psi alone has no physical
meaning, and it can be negative.

91
00:04:27,721 --> 00:04:30,426
Psi squared gives the probability density.

92
00:04:31,076 --> 00:04:32,472
Where is this asked?

93
00:04:32,472 --> 00:04:36,752
As statement questions on psi, psi
squared, orbit and orbital, and the

94
00:04:36,752 --> 00:04:38,241
features of the model.

95
00:04:38,841 --> 00:04:40,081
Exam level now.

96
00:04:40,081 --> 00:04:44,169
Which statement about the quantum
mechanical model is correct?

97
00:04:44,169 --> 00:04:45,461
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:52,142
The answer is B.

99
00:04:52,142 --> 00:04:56,370
Psi squared is the probability
density, and it is never negative.

100
00:04:56,870 --> 00:04:57,886
A is wrong.

101
00:04:57,886 --> 00:05:00,332
Psi itself has no physical meaning.

102
00:05:00,832 --> 00:05:01,868
C is wrong.

103
00:05:01,868 --> 00:05:04,859
An orbital can hold at most two electrons.

104
00:05:05,359 --> 00:05:06,353
D is wrong.

105
00:05:06,353 --> 00:05:10,736
An orbital is a region of probability,
not a fixed circular path.

106
00:05:11,336 --> 00:05:12,797
Here is your recap.

107
00:05:12,797 --> 00:05:14,298
Screenshot this card.

108
00:05:17,498 --> 00:05:20,335
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:20,735 --> 00:05:21,635
One.

110
00:05:21,199 --> 00:05:24,472
What is the difference between
an orbit and an orbital?

111
00:05:25,272 --> 00:05:26,172
Two.

112
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:31,129
Which tells us the probability of
finding an electron, psi or psi squared?

113
00:05:31,929 --> 00:05:32,829
Three.

114
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:35,455
How many electrons can one orbital hold?

115
00:05:36,355 --> 00:05:40,078
Next time: how does an atom
label each of its orbitals?

116
00:05:40,078 --> 00:05:42,979
We find out in orbitals
and quantum numbers.
